{
  "id": "Compound::DB00620 - Disease::DOID:7148",
  "verbalization": "Tiamcinolone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is linked to Rheumatoid Arthritis through its inclusion in the class of corticosteroids, which includes Hydrocortisone. This relationship is supported by multiple paths that establish a direct or indirect causal link between Triamcinolone and the disease. The lowest common ancestor of Triamcinolone and Hydrocortisone is the 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, suggesting a shared biological pathway. The evidence indicates that Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid, is known to treat Rheumatoid Arthritis, and since Triamcinolone is a member of the same class, it is plausible that it exerts similar therapeutic effects. The integration of these paths highlights a potential mechanism by which Triamcinolone may contribute to the treatment of Rheumatoid Arthritis, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
